  "output": {
    "answer": "The jitter numbers I quote — drops per 100 answers going 35.8 to 15.4 to 0.14 — came from event logs in E19, measured against a control, not from someone watching a screen. That's consistent with how I ran gated experiments generally: gates were judged on frozen, offline data, so no verdict needed a live stream. Separately, in the live card-scout demo I do say 'watch the card, it identifies, and it holds' — but that's me narrating a demo in the moment, not the mechanism that established the jitter fix. The record doesn't show a person's screen-watching as the criterion for the jitter numbers.",
